<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Mamluk Iraq</h1><p class="st-meta">1704 CE – 1831 CE · capital Baghdad</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The rule of Georgian mamluk pashas over Baghdad and Basra, who governed Iraq almost independently of the Ottoman sultan for over a century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1704 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-pasha-of-baghdad">Hasan Pasha</a> governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. He and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-pasha-of-baghdad">Ahmad Pasha</a> built a household of slave soldiers, mostly from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a>, trained for war and administration. In 1749 one of these mamluks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulayman-abu-layla-pasha">Sulayman Abu Layla</a>, became pasha, and mamluk officers then ruled Baghdad, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrizor">Shahrizor</a> in succession.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The pashas paid tribute and recognised the sultan but chose their successors from their own households. They fought Persian invasions, Arab tribes such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/muntafiq-confederation">Muntafiq confederation</a>, Kurdish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> and Wahhabi raiders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-suleyman-pasha">Büyük Süleyman Pasha</a> (1780 to 1802) restored order, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawud-pasha">Dawud Pasha</a> (1817 to 1831) introduced reforms, factories and a modern-style army, and dealt with the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company's</a> residents.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1831 plague and floods devastated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, and the Ottoman envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-riza-pasha-al-laz">Ali Rıza Pasha</a> deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawud-pasha">Dawud Pasha</a>, restoring direct Ottoman rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mamluk-iraq.jpg" alt="Mamluk Iraq" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Dbl2010 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Ottoman_Empire_(eight_pointed_star).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1704 CE – 1831 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Baghdad</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hasan Pasha, Büyük Süleyman Pasha, Dawud Pasha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ottoman Turkish, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mamluk dynasty of Iraq, Georgian Mamluks of Baghdad, Mamluk Pashalik of Baghdad</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1768&amp;focus=mamluk-iraq" aria-label="Mamluk Iraq on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5814 968 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mamluk Iraq lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6134 1224L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6134 1224h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Baghdad. <a href="/atlas?year=1768&amp;focus=mamluk-iraq">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

